    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1820</span><span class="rf-text">A regiment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> under the Carbonaro Guglielmo Pepe mutinies and wins a constitution that Austrian troops soon overturn</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The Milanese expel the Austrian garrison in the Five Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a> declares war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> is proclaimed on 9 February and falls to a French force on 29 June, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> sails from Quarto with about a thousand volunteers and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">The first Italian Parliament proclaims Victor Emmanuel King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 17 March</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1866</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> allies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and receives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1870</span><span class="rf-text">The Italian army breaches the Aurelian Walls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia">Porta Pia</a> and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri.jpg" alt="Dante Alighieri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bargello_-_Kapelle_Fresko_2a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was unified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in the latter part of the third century BCE and, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, lost its unity for centuries. It gradually developed into a system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in the south and the Papal States in the centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> and Guicciardini expressed a desire to see Italy become a unified nation, and in 1764 Gian Rinaldo Carli's Della Patria degli Italiani reflected a sense of Italian national identity.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> of 1792–97 set up a series of client republics. French rule destroyed the old structures of feudalism in Italy and introduced modern ideas and efficient legal authority, and it provided much of the intellectual force and social capital that fuelled unification movements for decades. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> fell, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1814–15 restored the pre-Napoleonic patchwork of governments, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Lombardy–Venetia</a> directly and the duchies of Parma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Tuscany indirectly.</p><p>Denis Mack Smith argues that few people in 1830 believed an Italian nation might even exist: there were eight states with distinct laws, and the common people spoke mutually unintelligible dialects, wanted good government rather than self-government and had welcomed the French as more efficient than their native dynasties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Mazzini" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Mazzini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement's supporters held three ideals of unification. In 1842 the Piedmontese priest Vincenzo Gioberti suggested a confederation of Italian states under the leadership of the pope. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-cattaneo">Carlo Cattaneo</a> wanted a federal republic, which proved too extreme for most nationalists. Cesare Balbo proposed the middle position, a confederation of separate Italian states led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Mazzini, while in prison, formulated a programme for a free, independent and republican nation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as its capital, and his society <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> took the mottos "God and People" and "Union, Strength and Freedom".</p><p>The Carbonari, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> that spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, sought to defeat tyranny and establish constitutional government. They took their principles of liberty, equality and fraternity from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and were strongly anticlerical. Italian exiles abroad, immersed in European ideas, called for a masculine response to what Europeans saw as Italian vices, and they fashioned their image of the future nation on the standards of European nationalism.</p><p>After the defeats of 1848–1849 the patriots drew three lessons. They had to expel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> first, even at the price of whatever <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> asked in return. The pope was an enemy and could never lead a united Italy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republicanism">Republicanism</a> was too weak a force, so unification had to rest on a strong monarchy, in practice Piedmont under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>. The movement's later motto was "Free from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to the Adriatic".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti.jpg" alt="Ciro Menotti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti">Ciro Menotti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciro_Menotti_-_Malatesta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1820 a regiment of the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a>, commanded by the Carbonaro Guglielmo Pepe, mutinied and King Ferdinand I agreed to a constitution. The revolutionaries failed to court popular support and fell to Austrian troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-alliance">Holy Alliance</a>, after which Ferdinand abolished the constitution and persecuted known revolutionaries. In 1821 troops at Alessandria adopted the green, white and red tricolore in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> revolt led by Santorre di Santarosa, but King Charles Felix disavowed the constitution on his return and Di Santarosa's troops were defeated. In 1831 the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti">Ciro Menotti</a> with Austrian help and Menotti was executed. The Austrian army then slowly crushed the risings in the Papal Legations and Parma.</p><p>In 1844 Attilio and Emilio Bandiera, members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a>, landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crotone">Crotone</a> with about twenty men, were betrayed and, with nine companions, were executed by firing squad. In 1848 revolts began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and on 18 March the Milanese and Venetians rose; the Austrian garrison was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> after five days of street fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> but was defeated at Custoza on 24 July and at Novara on 23 March 1849. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> proclaimed on 9 February 1849 fell to a French force on 29 June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> surrendered to the Austrians on 24 August.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Garibaldi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Giuseppe_Garibaldi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Salvatore Lo Forte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> at Marseille after his release from prison in 1831 and became Chief Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in March 1849. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, was sentenced to death after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> uprising in 1834, escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and spent fourteen years in exile, where he learned the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. On 6 May 1860 he sailed from Quarto near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> with about a thousand volunteers, landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsala">Marsala</a>, won at Calatafimi on 13 May and declared himself dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the name of Victor Emmanuel. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 7 September and later handed over his dictatorial power to the king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a> (1810–1861), a modernizer interested in agrarian improvements, banks, railways and free trade, became prime minister of Piedmont in 1852. He brought the kingdom into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1855 and found an ally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> in 1859. He died in June 1861; when given the last rites he purportedly said: "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> is made. All is safe."</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> succeeded his father Charles Albert in 1849 and was given the epithet Father of the Fatherland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> had at first appeared a reformer and granted a constitution to the Papal States on 21 February 1848, but he turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and nationalism and fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in November 1848.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porta-pia.jpg" alt="Porta Pia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia">Porta Pia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porta_Pia_-_internal_side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Monticiano, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In April 1859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> found an ally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and the Second War of Italian Independence began. The Austrians lost at Magenta and Solferino, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villafranca">Armistice of Villafranca</a> on 12 July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> and Mantua. Sardinia annexed central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 22 March 1860 and gave Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 24 March. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s campaign in the south, the first Italian Parliament proclaimed Victor Emmanuel King of Italy on 17 March 1861. Italy received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> in 1866 after a referendum in which 69 of more than 642,000 ballots opposed annexation; historians suggest it was held under military pressure. On 20 September 1870 the Bersaglieri entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through a breach at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia">Porta Pia</a>, where 49 Italian soldiers and four officers and nineteen papal troops died. Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> were annexed after a plebiscite on 2 October.</p><p>Historians have stressed the cost. Martin Clark says, "It was Piedmontization all around": the constitution, tax rates, officials and top military posts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s, and Cavour's promises of regional and local government were broken in 1861. The first decade saw savage insurrections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> region. The pope ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> not to co-operate with the new government, a decision fully reversed only in 1929.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci.jpg" alt="Antonio Gramsci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci">Antonio Gramsci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gramsci.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Italian unification is still debated. Massimo d'Azeglio said: "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> has been made. Now it remains to make Italians." Francesco Saverio Nitti criticised the new state for ignoring the economic differences between north and south, which he held to be the cause of the southern question. Gaetano Salvemini held that a lack of understanding and action by politicians let corruption and organised crime flourish in the south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci">Antonio Gramsci</a> criticised the limited presence of the masses in politics and the lack of land reform. Denis Mack Smith, Christopher Duggan and Lucy Riall are among the authors who continue to review its successes and failures.</p><p>Many Italian speakers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and Venezia Giulia remained outside the kingdom, which gave rise to Italian irredentism. The annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> in 1918 is often considered the culmination of the movement, and some historians see the Risorgimento as continuing to the Treaty of Rapallo in 1920. Historians regard the "mutilated victory" rhetoric that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> adopted as a political myth.</p><p>Italy celebrates the anniversary of the unification on 17 March. Il Canto degli Italiani, first performed publicly in 1847, has been the national anthem since 1946. Historians disagree on how political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>'s operas were: Franco Della Peruta stresses his patriotic intent, while Roger Parker argues that nationalistic historians exaggerated it.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, or Risorgimento, joined the states of the Italian Peninsula into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> between the failed revolts of the 1820s and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1870. It rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s monarchy, French and Prussian wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s volunteers.</em></p>

